WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.712 --> 00:00:12.592
In the 7th century CE, 
one man started a chain of events

00:00:12.592 --> 00:00:14.927
that would change
the world order for good.

00:00:15.428 --> 00:00:19.474
The prophet Muhammad united the people
of the Arabian Peninsula

00:00:19.474 --> 00:00:21.476
through the formation of Islam.

00:00:21.893 --> 00:00:25.354
These people included both 
nomadic Bedouin tribes

00:00:25.354 --> 00:00:28.941
and the inhabitants of oasis cities
like Mecca and Medina.

00:00:29.317 --> 00:00:34.280
Until Muhammad’s time, the region 
hadn’t been considered a serious match

00:00:34.280 --> 00:00:37.950
for the powerful neighboring 
Persian and Byzantine empires.

00:00:38.367 --> 00:00:42.330
But the alliance Muhammad formed 
was political as well as religious,

00:00:42.330 --> 00:00:45.374
an empire with Medina 
as its political heart

00:00:45.374 --> 00:00:47.210
and a force to be reckoned with.

00:00:47.293 --> 00:00:49.587
Muhammad was a one-of-a-kind leader.

00:00:49.796 --> 00:00:53.925
He had been a member of the Quraysh,
the tribe that controlled Mecca.

00:00:54.175 --> 00:00:55.968
After Muhammad’s death,

00:00:55.968 --> 00:00:58.930
those close to him deliberated
who should succeed him—

00:00:59.263 --> 00:01:00.807
a contentious question.

00:01:01.099 --> 00:01:03.768
Abū Bakr, Muhammad’s father-in-law,

00:01:03.768 --> 00:01:07.605
emerged victorious and became 
the new caliph, or successor.

00:01:08.189 --> 00:01:13.111
Over the next 30 years, four caliphs,
all from Muhammad's tribe,

00:01:13.111 --> 00:01:15.613
conquered vast areas beyond Arabia,

00:01:15.905 --> 00:01:20.076
including their mighty neighbors, 
the Persians and the Byzantines.

00:01:20.368 --> 00:01:24.247
But as the empire expanded, 
dissent within it grew

00:01:24.247 --> 00:01:26.040
and a civil war erupted.

00:01:26.457 --> 00:01:29.836
The fourth caliph, Ali, was assassinated.

00:01:30.211 --> 00:01:33.714
Afterwards, the Umayyad Dynasty
came to power.

00:01:34.048 --> 00:01:36.884
The Umayyads were from the same tribe
as Muhammad,

00:01:36.884 --> 00:01:38.886
but from a different, rival clan.

00:01:39.220 --> 00:01:43.391
They extended the empire’s reach
from present-day Spain to India

00:01:43.516 --> 00:01:45.560
and made Damascus their capital.

00:01:46.060 --> 00:01:49.939
But an empire this vast, 
full of many different peoples,

00:01:49.939 --> 00:01:52.358
was at risk of conflict and fracture.

00:01:52.692 --> 00:01:57.864
The Umayyads stabilized it by replacing
the ruling elite in conquered territories

00:01:57.864 --> 00:01:59.490
with Muslim officials,

00:01:59.490 --> 00:02:03.911
while largely allowing the day-to-day
customs of local populations—

00:02:03.911 --> 00:02:07.081
including their religious preferences—
to continue.

00:02:07.582 --> 00:02:10.418
Arabic was used 
as the administrative language,

00:02:10.543 --> 00:02:13.462
unifying political affairs
across the empire,

00:02:13.838 --> 00:02:17.633
but people continued to speak and write
local languages, too.

00:02:18.342 --> 00:02:22.680
Still, many in the empire were
dissatisfied with Umayyad rule

00:02:22.680 --> 00:02:25.141
and questioned the dynasty's legitimacy.

00:02:25.433 --> 00:02:28.561
The Abbasid family capitalized
on these sentiments,

00:02:28.728 --> 00:02:32.190
promoting themselves as more direct
descendants of the prophet,

00:02:32.315 --> 00:02:36.360
though their actual relation to Muhammad
was more tenuous than they claimed.

00:02:36.611 --> 00:02:40.489
They overthrew the Umayyad caliphate
in 750 CE,

00:02:40.489 --> 00:02:43.784
becoming the second great dynasty
of the Islamic Empire.

00:02:44.785 --> 00:02:47.330
To establish themselves as the new rulers,

00:02:47.330 --> 00:02:49.790
they relocated the capital once more,

00:02:49.790 --> 00:02:52.627
this time building a new city: Baghdad.

00:02:52.919 --> 00:02:57.256
Under Abbasid rule, the elite 
enjoyed a lifestyle of luxury,

00:02:57.256 --> 00:03:01.302
thanks to extensive trade networks
that brought both products and people

00:03:01.302 --> 00:03:03.846
from all over the known world to Baghdad.

00:03:04.263 --> 00:03:10.436
Byzantine, Persian, Indian and Arab
cultures and knowledge intermingled,

00:03:10.603 --> 00:03:13.856
leading to artistic and scientific
advancement.

00:03:14.065 --> 00:03:17.568
The caliph was wealthy and powerful
beyond imagination.

00:03:18.236 --> 00:03:20.488
But there was never a clear line 
of succession

00:03:20.488 --> 00:03:23.032
dictating who would become
the next caliph.

00:03:23.366 --> 00:03:26.702
Any male relative of the former caliph 
was eligible,

00:03:26.702 --> 00:03:30.915
so brothers, nephews, and uncles 
fought to gain power.

00:03:31.290 --> 00:03:36.963
Within the court, army officers, wives, 
concubines, and government officials

00:03:36.963 --> 00:03:39.423
all demanded their share of the treasury.

00:03:39.840 --> 00:03:43.511
Because the caliph depended 
on his entourage to stay in power,

00:03:43.761 --> 00:03:48.474
each transition of rulership opened
the doors for favoritism and corruption.

00:03:48.641 --> 00:03:49.934
Outside the court,

00:03:49.934 --> 00:03:52.770
many questioned the legitimacy
of the caliph,

00:03:52.770 --> 00:03:56.232
noting that the caliph’s religious duty
to moral excellence

00:03:56.232 --> 00:03:59.318
was at odds with the court’s 
decadent displays of wealth.

00:03:59.610 --> 00:04:03.698
In 1258 CE, the Mongols
approached Baghdad.

00:04:03.990 --> 00:04:07.618
They encountered little resistance 
as they thoroughly destroyed the city.

00:04:07.952 --> 00:04:10.705
Legend has it that they rolled
the caliph in a rug

00:04:10.705 --> 00:04:12.748
and had horses trample him to death,

00:04:12.999 --> 00:04:16.210
and that the Tigris River ran
black from the ink of the manuscripts

00:04:16.210 --> 00:04:17.837
that were thrown into it.

00:04:18.587 --> 00:04:22.049
The siege of Baghdad laid bare
a longstanding reality:

00:04:22.300 --> 00:04:26.053
for centuries, the caliphs had
ruled mostly symbolically.

00:04:26.512 --> 00:04:29.724
Local leaders throughout the empire
had grown more powerful,

00:04:29.724 --> 00:04:31.726
and they refused to pay taxes,

00:04:31.726 --> 00:04:34.395
spending the money on their own courts
instead.

00:04:34.979 --> 00:04:38.232
The time of one united Islamic Empire
was over,

00:04:38.524 --> 00:04:41.819
but its influence 
through written and spoken Arabic,

00:04:41.819 --> 00:04:45.406
Islam, and the ideas 
of its greatest intellectuals,

00:04:45.406 --> 00:04:47.908
left a lasting mark on the world.

